<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 53 0 R
/T (Aim:  Implementation and Time analysis of linear and binary search algorithm.  Theory:  The Linear Search and Binary Search algorithms are the two fundamental techniques used to find a target value within a collection of data. While Linear Search checks elements sequentially and works on any dataset, Binary Search uses a divide-and-conquer strategy but strictly requires the data to be sorted beforehand.  Algorithm  Algorithm for Linear Search  Start.  Read the array A of n elements and the search element key.  Set i = 0.  Compare A[i] with key. If they are equal, display the position and stop.  Otherwise, increment i and repeat Step 4 until i = n.  If the element is not found after checking all elements, display "Element not found".  Stop.  Algorithm for Binary Search  Note: Binary Search requires the array to be sorted.  Start.  Read the sorted array A of n elements and the search element key. )
/E (Aim:  Implementation and Time analysis of linear and binary search algorithm.  Theory:  The Linear Search and Binary Search algorithms are the two fundamental techniques used to find a target value within a collection of data. While Linear Search checks elements sequentially and works on any dataset, Binary Search uses a divide-and-conquer strategy but strictly requires the data to be sorted beforehand.  Algorithm  Algorithm for Linear Search  Start.  Read the array A of n elements and the search element key.  Set i = 0.  Compare A[i] with key. If they are equal, display the position and stop.  Otherwise, increment i and repeat Step 4 until i = n.  If the element is not found after checking all elements, display "Element not found".  Stop.  Algorithm for Binary Search  Note: Binary Search requires the array to be sorted.  Start.  Read the sorted array A of n elements and the search element key. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
